  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Tennessee has received $10.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 19,181 awards, with an average award size of $557K, and obligations fell 2.5% year over year. Current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date. The Department of Energy is the dominant buyer, obligating $6.9B, while the Department of Defense obligated $1.5B and the Department of Justice obligated $721M. Contractors tied to energy and remediation dominate the vendor list: CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC received $3.1B and UT-BATTELLE LLC received $2.6B. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $5.8B, followed by Remediation Services at $724M and Engineering Services at $680M. The data shows federal spending concentrated in energy, cleanup, facilities support, and related construction and security services, with a small decline in total obligations compared with the prior year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 2.5% year over year. Total obligations in FY2026 to date are $10.7B, compared with prior fiscal years that ranged from $10.8B to $11.4B. The average award size in the current year is $557K, and the state has recorded 19,181 awards so far."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top buyers and vendors in Tennessee?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy is the largest buyer at $6.9B, followed by the Department of Defense at $1.5B and the Department of Justice at $721M. CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC is the largest vendor at $3.1B, followed by UT-BATTELLE LLC at $2.6B and UNITED CLEANUP OAK RIDGE LLC at $559M."
      }
    ]
